Day 1 Project Report – Project Setup & UI Development
Project Setup & User Interface

On Day 1, the primary focus was on setting up the project structure and developing the basic user interface for the Login and Registration modules.
The objective was to create a clean, responsive, and user-friendly interface that can be extended with backend functionality in the upcoming stages.
First, the project environment was configured and the required folders and files were organized properly.
A structured project architecture was created to separate components, pages, assets, and other resources.
This structure will help maintain the project efficiently and make future development easier.
The Login page was developed with input fields for essential user credentials such as email/username and password.
Proper labels, buttons, spacing, and basic validation considerations were included to provide a simple and clear user experience.
A Registration page was also created to allow new users to enter their required information and create an account.
The interface was designed consistently with the Login page so that users can easily navigate between the two pages.
The project also includes separate interface considerations for User and Admin login.
The purpose of this design is to provide different access levels based on the type of user.
The Admin interface can later be connected with administrative features, while the User interface can be connected with regular user functionalities.

Day 2 – User Authentication
On Day 2 of the project, the main focus was on implementing User Authentication.
The objective was to create a secure and user-friendly authentication system that allows users to register, log in, and log out of the application.
Authentication is an important part of any web application because it protects user-specific information and ensures that only authorized users can access certain features.

User Registration
The first task completed was the user registration functionality.
A registration form was created where users can enter their basic details, including username/email and password.
Basic validation was implemented to ensure that required fields are not empty and that the entered information follows the expected format.
The system also checks whether the username or email already exists before creating a new account.

User Login
The second task was implementing the user login functionality.
Registered users can log in using their username or email and password.
The entered credentials are validated against the stored user information.
If the credentials are correct, the user is successfully authenticated and can access the application's protected features. 
If the credentials are incorrect, an appropriate error message is displayed.

User Logout
A logout feature was also implemented. 
It allows authenticated users to securely end their session.
After logout, the user is redirected appropriately and cannot access protected pages without logging in again.

Basic Validation
Basic input validation was added to improve reliability and user experience.
Empty fields, invalid email formats, incorrect credentials, and duplicate accounts are handled appropriately.

Day 3 – Admin Authentication & Dashboards
On Day 3, I implemented a separate Admin Authentication system to provide secure access to administrative features.
Admin login was developed using predefined admin credentials, ensuring that only authorized administrators could access the Admin Dashboard.
I also created separate User and Admin Dashboards based on their respective roles.
Protected routes were implemented to prevent unauthorized users from accessing restricted pages.
If a user attempted to access an admin-only page without proper authentication, the system redirected the user to the appropriate login or dashboard page.
This improved the overall security and role management of the application.

Outcome: Working Admin Login, User Dashboard, Admin Dashboard, and protected routes.

Day 4 – Database Integration

On Day 4, I integrated the application with a database such as MongoDB/MySQL/Firebase for persistent data storage.
A Users collection/table was created to store important user information such as name, email, password-related data, role, and account status.
I implemented functionality to save newly registered user details into the database and fetch existing user records when required. 
This replaced temporary/local data handling with a structured database system and enabled the application to maintain user information efficiently.
Outcome: Connected database with successfully stored and retrieved user records.

